          {
            "turn": 2,
            "text": "*Sets down the brush and squints at the stranger, brow furrowed.*\n\n\"English,\" you say — I have never heard such a word in all my years. Is it the tongue of some far land, like the speech of the Jurchen beyond the northern river, or of the island folk across the sea? I know our own letters, and the writing of Han besides, but this I cannot make out at all. From what country does this speech of yours come?"
          },
          {
            "turn": 3,
            "text": "*Sets the brush down slowly, then lifts the sheet of new letters and turns it toward the stranger.*\n\nI see — so the speech of this land is closed to you, and only this English tongue opens it. Strange to think a man may walk the streets of Hanseong and yet not read a single mark of what is spoken there. When this script of mine is spread abroad, I had hoped no one in the realm would be left standing outside the letters — but the world, it seems, is wider than the realm. Sit, then, and we shall go on in your tongue; I would hear how such a speech as this is written down."
          },
          {
            "turn": 4,
            "text": "*Frowns slightly, turning the unfamiliar word over.*\n\n\"Weekends\" — that I do not know. In this palace the days are not divided so; there are court days and fasting days, and days when the rains fail and I set aside my wine. What I love best is a long night with the scholars of the Hall of Worthies, reading until my eyes blur, and a table with a good meat dish upon it."
          }
        ]
      },
